  <details class="sec" id="scholarships">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>Scholarships and Funding</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">Every deadline below was read on the awarding body&rsquo;s own page, and each entry shows the date it was last verified: Craig Huffman, EAA and AOPA on <strong>2 October 2026</strong>, Space Grant on 25 September, the rest on 4 September. The eligibility year is stated on every entry. Confirmed-open ones come first, soonest deadline first. Anything past its deadline was removed.</p>

    <div class="note">The move: apply to <strong>several</strong>, not one. Write one strong piece about building your own drone and your aerospace projects, then tailor it per application. The Bold.org ones all run off a single free profile, so the second and third applications take minutes. <strong>Nearest deadline is Saturday 10 October, Craig Huffman, a 400 to 600 word essay. EAA (due 16 December) and AOPA (due 31 December) both opened 1 October.</strong> Be Bold closed 30 September and came off the page.</div>

    <a class="card" href="https://bold.org/scholarships/craig-huffman-memorial-scholarship/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">&#9733; Craig Huffman Memorial Scholarship</p><span class="pay event">$4,000, two winners</span></div>
      <p class="org">Huffman Family, via Bold.org</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: <strong>Saturday 10 October 2026</strong> &middot; verified open 2 Oct &middot; eligibility: &ldquo;Any high school student, undergraduate, graduate student, or trade school student who is pursuing a career in aviation&rdquo;</p>
      <p class="why">The soonest award that is about your path, and the one to write first. The prompt, from their page: how you became interested in aviation and how you exemplify leadership, 400 to 600 words. Two winners at $2,000, announced 11 November. Lead with the drone you built and the Part 107.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Craig Huffman page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://bold.org/scholarships/mad-genius-scholarship/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">&#9733; Mad Genius Scholarship</p><span class="pay event">$8,000, eight winners</span></div>
      <p class="org">FoodStory Brands, via Bold.org</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: 1 November 2026 &middot; verified open 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: any education level, so a first-year qualifies</p>
      <p class="why">Built to reward inventive, out-of-the-box builders. A student who designed and built his own drone is exactly who this is for. Eight winners at a thousand each, so the odds are better than the single-winner awards. Tell the build story.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Mad Genius page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.smartscholarship.org/smart" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">SMART Scholarship-for-Service (Department of Defense)</p><span class="pay event">Full tuition + stipend</span></div>
      <p class="org">U.S. Department of Defense</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: first Friday in December, <strong>4 December 2026</strong> at 5 p.m. ET &middot; applications open August to December &middot; eligibility: undergraduates, including first-years, in an approved STEM discipline</p>
      <p class="why">The largest amount of money on this page by a wide margin: full tuition, a yearly stipend, a paid summer internship at a DoD facility, and guaranteed employment after graduation. <strong>Read the trade carefully</strong>, because it is a scholarship-for-service and it commits you to work for the Department of Defense year for year after you graduate. Their eligibility detail sits behind a portal, so open the page and read the requirements yourself before you start.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the SMART Scholarship site &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.eaa.org/eaa/learn-to-fly/scholarships/post-secondary-scholarships" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">&#9733; EAA Aviation Foundation post-secondary scholarships</p><span class="pay event">$237,000 to 39 students last cycle</span></div>
      <p class="org">Experimental Aircraft Association</p>
      <p class="loc">Open now &middot; deadline <strong>16 December 2026</strong> &middot; verified open 2 Oct &middot; eligibility: current college students in an aviation-focused program, explicitly including aeronautics engineering, minimum 2.5 GPA, U.S. or Canadian resident</p>
      <p class="why">Their page: &ldquo;The 2026-2027 scholarship application cycle is now open. All applications are due by December 16, 2026,&rdquo; and &ldquo;one application is used for ALL scholarships.&rdquo; EAA membership is not required but members get priority. Two named awards fit a first-year directly: the <strong>David Mineck Memorial Scholarship</strong>, &ldquo;post-secondary education in the field of aerospace,&rdquo; and the <strong>Jack Burnham Fund</strong>, for students at a four-year college in aeronautics or engineering. It needs a transcript and one non-relative reference who has to submit before the deadline, so ask that person in November.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the EAA scholarships page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.aopa.org/training-and-safety/students/aopa-flight-training-scholarships" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">AOPA Foundation scholarships</p><span class="pay event">$250 to $14,000</span></div>
      <p class="org">Aircraft Owners and Pilots Association Foundation</p>
      <p class="loc">Open now &middot; Fall window <strong>1 October to 31 December 2026</strong>, 11:59 p.m. ET &middot; verified open 2 Oct &middot; eligibility: current AOPA members resident in the U.S., any year</p>
      <p class="why">Their page now reads &ldquo;Now Accepting Applications. Exclusively for AOPA Members.&rdquo; One general application matches you to every award you qualify for. Most of the money is flight training, so read the fit honestly, but one listed award is aimed squarely at you: the <strong>Edward B. Shafer Future of Flight Scholarship</strong>, $5,000, for students &ldquo;currently enrolled at an accredited university or college and pursuing a career in aviation, including &hellip; aerospace engineering.&rdquo; Membership has a cost; the page offers a free trial. Transcript and references are required. Decisions by 15 April.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the AOPA scholarships page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.smeef.org/scholarships/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">SME Education Foundation scholarships</p><span class="pay event">$2,500 to $20,000</span></div>
      <p class="org">SME Education Foundation</p>
      <p class="loc">Window: <strong>1 November 2026 to 1 February 2027</strong> &middot; verified 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: current college students in technical fields supporting manufacturing, any year</p>
      <p class="why">Their page states it directly: &ldquo;Scholarship applications are accepted annually from November 1 &ndash; February 1. By submitting one application, you will be considered for every scholarship for which you are eligible.&rdquo; More than sixty distinct programs, merit and need-based, renewable and one-time. Register the free account now so they email you when it opens.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the SME Education Foundation page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/scholarships/cuboulder-scholarship-app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">&#9733; CU Boulder Scholarship Application</p><span class="pay event">One form, many awards</span></div>
      <p class="org">Office of Scholarship Services, University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="loc">Opens <strong>1 December 2026</strong> for 2027-28 &middot; most awards close between 15 February and 15 March 2027 &middot; verified 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: continuing undergraduates, which is you from December</p>
      <p class="why">The highest ratio of money to effort anywhere on this page, and the one people forget. One general application puts you in front of merit awards, need-based awards, and <strong>school and college-specific scholarships</strong>, which is where the engineering and Smead aerospace money lives. It opens 1 December, three months out, and takes an evening. Do not miss the March window.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the CU Boulder Scholarship Application &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://vtol.org/vff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">&#9733; Vertical Flight Foundation scholarship</p><span class="pay event">$4,000 to $6,000</span></div>
      <p class="org">The Vertical Flight Society</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: <strong>1 February 2027</strong>, stated on their page as &ldquo;2027 Deadline: Feb. 1&rdquo; &middot; verified 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: full-time students at any university worldwide, any year, any nationality</p>
      <p class="why">Made for students in rotorcraft, eVTOL and drone flight, which is your wheelhouse, and their eligibility is unusually open: &ldquo;open to full-time students attending a university or college located anywhere in the world and regardless of a student&rsquo;s nationality,&rdquo; solely merit-based. Smaller applicant pool than the giant national funds. The top applicant gets the $6,000 Bell award.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the VFF scholarship page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://spie.org/membership/student-hub/scholarships/optics-and-photonics-education-scholarships" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">SPIE Optics and Photonics Education Scholarships</p><span class="pay event">$3,000 to $5,000</span></div>
      <p class="org">SPIE, the international society for optics and photonics</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: <strong>15 February 2027</strong>, 5 p.m. Pacific &middot; verified 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: SPIE student members, undergraduates judged against similar backgrounds</p>
      <p class="why">Roughly seventy awards a year, and undergraduates are judged relative to other applicants with similar educational backgrounds rather than against PhD students. Student membership is about twenty dollars and has to stay current through the review. Optics is the heart of every space instrument LASP builds, so this is closer to your field than it first looks.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the SPIE scholarships page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://bold.org/scholarships/stitt-family-aeronautics-aerospace-and-aviation-scholarship/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">&#9733; Stitt Family Aeronautics &amp; Aerospace Scholarship</p><span class="pay event">$30,000, three winners</span></div>
      <p class="org">Stitt Family, via Bold.org</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: <strong>10 March 2027</strong> &middot; verified open 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: undergraduates studying aerospace or aeronautics in Colorado and a few nearby states, GPA 3.3 or higher, financial need</p>
      <p class="why">Your bullseye, and the biggest single award you are clearly eligible for. Colorado plus aerospace major plus undergraduate is the whole target profile. Past winners were drone builders with a Part 107, which is precisely your file. Short piece about wanting to be at the forefront of aerospace. Lead with the build.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Stitt scholarship page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://bold.org/scholarships/redefining-victory-scholarship/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Redefining Victory Scholarship</p><span class="pay event">$20,000, eight winners</span></div>
      <p class="org">Chris and Mary Carnduff, via Bold.org</p>
      <p class="loc">Deadline: <strong>30 March 2027</strong> &middot; verified open 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: any education level, so a first-year qualifies</p>
      <p class="why">Eight winners at $2,500 each, open to any education level, long runway. Another short one from the same Bold profile, so it costs you a few minutes once the profile exists.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Redefining Victory page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.elevatescholarship.org/scholarship" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Elevate Scholarship, pays for your FAA Part 107 exam</p><span class="pay event">Rolling</span></div>
      <p class="org">National Center for Autonomous Technologies, with Prime Air, DroneDeploy and AUVSI</p>
      <p class="loc">Rolling, apply anytime &middot; verified open 4 Sept &middot; eligibility: 16 to 24 and enrolled in a UAS, aviation or Part 107 course at an accredited school</p>
      <p class="why">You already hold the Part 107, so this one is only worth your time if you take a UAS or aviation course at CU, in which case it covers exam and training costs and decisions come back in about two weeks. Their application requires an IACRA account and a PSI account first, so set those up before you start. Kept on the page as a low-effort option tied to a course, not as a priority.</p>
