  <details class="sec" id="news">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Today in Aerospace</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">Seven headlines worth knowing cold, pulled from Spaceflight Now, LASP, NASA and Colorado sources. Read one a day. Links opened 2 October 2026.</p>

    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/09/28/starship-returns-to-earth-rocket-splashes-down-north-of-hawaii-after-three-hour-flight/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Starship reached orbit for the first time</p></div>
      <p class="org">Spaceflight Now, 28 September</p>
      <p class="why">After thirteen suborbital test flights, Flight 14 made it to a 180-mile orbit on 28 September and deployed <strong>26 Starlink V3 satellites</strong>, the first operational payload Starship has ever carried. It was not clean: an upper-stage Raptor shut down early on the climb, and flight controllers chose to bring the ship down early, splashing it north of Hawaii after three hours instead of the planned six orbits. That decision under uncertainty is the most interesting engineering in the story. The Artemis Moon lander is a version of this vehicle.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the story &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/10/01/fresh-crew-takes-off-for-six-month-stay-aboard-space-station/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Crew-13 is on its way to the station</p></div>
      <p class="org">Spaceflight Now, 1 October</p>
      <p class="why">Commander Jessica Watkins, pilot Luke Delaney, cosmonaut Sergey Teteryatnikov and Canadian astronaut Joshua Kutryk launched on Thursday 1 October from Cape Canaveral, after a two-week slip to replace a leaking valve in the Dragon&rsquo;s propulsion system. The catch-up to the station took eight hours, a NASA record. Worth reading the paragraph on why every launch to the station has to wait for Earth&rsquo;s rotation to carry the pad into the station&rsquo;s orbital plane: it is orbital mechanics you will meet in class.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the story &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/09/29/nasa-plans-unpiloted-starliner-test-flight-at-end-of-year/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Boeing&rsquo;s Starliner gets one more uncrewed test</p></div>
      <p class="org">Spaceflight Now, 29 September</p>
      <p class="why">NASA and Boeing plan an uncrewed Starliner flight to the station in December or January, then a crewed flight in mid 2028. The fixes are a case study in propulsion engineering: redesigned thruster valve poppets, replaced valves on all twelve crew-module thrusters, changes to keep the thrusters from overheating. The 2024 mission that needed them turned a 10-day trip into 286 days. ULA, in Centennial, is working to certify Vulcan to carry it.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the story &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/09/24/nasa-announces-new-space-telescope-prima-to-launch-in-2033/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">NASA picked its next space telescope</p></div>
      <p class="org">Spaceflight Now, 24 September</p>
      <p class="why">PRIMA, a far-infrared observatory with a cryogenically cooled 1.8-metre telescope, moved into Phase B development with a cost cap of $1.2 billion and a 2033 launch. It opens a new class of mid-size NASA missions called Probe Explorers. Launching in 2033 means it gets designed and built while you are in school and early in your career.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the story &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://lasp.colorado.edu/2026/09/23/lasp-solar-scientist-ryan-french-receives-2026-agu-athelstan-spilhaus-award-for-public-engagement/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">A LASP solar physicist won a national award for explaining the Sun</p></div>
      <p class="org">LASP, CU Boulder, 23 September</p>
      <p class="why">Ryan French, who studies solar flares at LASP, received the American Geophysical Union&rsquo;s 2026 Spilhaus Award for public engagement. A short read, and a good example of the lab you want to work in: the people there do the science and also explain it well, which is a skill every engineering interview quietly tests.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the LASP story &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://gazette.com/2026/09/11/colorado-at-risk-of-being-eclipsed-by-other-aerospace-hubs-reports-warn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Colorado is worried about losing its aerospace lead</p></div>
      <p class="org">The Gazette, on reports from the Colorado Chamber Foundation</p>
      <p class="why">Colorado aerospace supports <strong>more than 61,000 jobs and $14 billion of state GDP</strong>, and two new reports warn that Virginia, Florida, Oklahoma and Texas are growing faster. The strengths named are the ones you are walking into: a concentrated ecosystem, leading universities, and startups that grew into billion-dollar companies, York Space Systems, Voyager and Ursa Major. The state answered with tax incentives, $35 million of it to Ursa Major alone.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the reports coverage &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://oedit.colorado.gov/press-release/ursa-major-expands-aerospace-manufacturing-in-longmont-berthoud-poised-to-create-up" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">A rocket-engine plant just opened twenty minutes from campus</p></div>
      <p class="org">Colorado Office of Economic Development and International Trade</p>
      <p class="why">Read this as a map of where the jobs are going. Ursa Major opened a new manufacturing facility in <strong>Longmont</strong>, expanding avionics and in-space propulsion production alongside its Berthoud headquarters, with welding and manufacturing labs, an ISO 7 clean room and an environmental test lab. The wider expansion is poised to create up to 1,850 Colorado jobs.</p>
      <p class="go">Read the announcement &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

  </details>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <details class="sec" id="launches">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>Watch &middot; Upcoming Launches</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">The next launches and landings on the calendar, pulled from the Spaceflight Now schedule on 2 October 2026. Dates and times slip constantly, so confirm the day of. Most carry a free live webcast.</p>

    <div class="note">Where to watch live, free: <a class="inline" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#7fa6ff;text-decoration:none;">Spaceflight Now launch schedule</a> (the master list), <a class="inline" href="https://rocketlaunch.live/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#7fa6ff;text-decoration:none;">rocketlaunch.live</a> (clean countdowns), <a class="inline" href="https://plus.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#7fa6ff;text-decoration:none;">NASA+ / NASA TV</a> and the <a class="inline" href="https://www.spacex.com/launches/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#7fa6ff;text-decoration:none;">SpaceX webcast</a>.</div>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Crew-12 comes home</p><span class="pay event">Undock Mon 5 Oct &middot; splashdown Tue 6 Oct</span></div>
      <p class="org">NASA and SpaceX</p>
      <p class="loc">Pacific Ocean, off the southern California coast</p>
      <p class="why">The return half of this week&rsquo;s crew swap. Jessica Meir&rsquo;s crew leaves the station after 235 days and splashes down off California. Re-entry and parachutes are a harder problem than launch in a lot of ways, and NASA carries it live.</p>
      <p class="go">Follow NASA&rsquo;s station coverage &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Falcon 9 &middot; Starlink 15-25</p><span class="pay event">Sat 10 Oct</span></div>
      <p class="org">SpaceX</p>
      <p class="loc">SLC-4E, Vandenberg Space Force Base, California &middot; time to be set</p>
      <p class="why">A routine one, and that is the point: booster B1082 is flying for the <strong>25th time</strong> and will land on a droneship in the Pacific. Reuse at that count is what changed the economics of the whole industry.</p>
      <p class="go">Confirm the time and watch &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">H3 &middot; Martian Moons eXploration (MMX)</p><span class="pay event">NET Mon 19 Oct</span></div>
      <p class="org">JAXA and Mitsubishi Heavy Industries</p>
      <p class="loc">Tanegashima Space Center, Japan &middot; 1:41 p.m. your time on 19 October</p>
      <p class="why">The most interesting thing on this list, and it launches on a Monday afternoon your time. Japan is sending a spacecraft to <strong>Phobos, a moon of Mars, to land, collect a sample and bring it home</strong>, with the sample due back around 2031. It would be the first sample return from the Martian system.</p>
      <p class="go">Confirm the time and watch &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Alpha Block 2 &middot; FLTA008</p><span class="pay event">NET Q4 2026</span></div>
      <p class="org">Firefly Aerospace</p>
      <p class="loc">SLC-2, Vandenberg Space Force Base, California</p>
      <p class="why">The debut of a stretched Alpha with larger liquid oxygen tanks on both stages. Firefly is on the internship watch list above, so this is a launch with a job connection.</p>
      <p class="go">Confirm the date &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Vulcan Centaur &middot; Dream Chaser 1</p><span class="pay event">NET Q4 2026</span></div>
      <p class="org">United Launch Alliance, flying Sierra Space&rsquo;s Dream Chaser</p>
      <p class="loc">SLC-41, Cape Canaveral Space Force Station, Florida</p>
      <p class="why">Your hometown launch, still with no firm date. The first flight of a winged cargo spaceplane built in Louisville, twenty minutes from campus, riding a rocket assembled in Centennial. Both companies are on the internship list above.</p>
      <p class="go">Confirm the date &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Atlas V &middot; Boeing Starliner-1</p><span class="pay event">December or January</span></div>
      <p class="org">Boeing, NASA and United Launch Alliance</p>
      <p class="loc">SLC-41, Cape Canaveral Space Force Station, Florida</p>
      <p class="why">The uncrewed test described in the news section above. If the fixed thrusters behave, it sets up a crewed flight in 2028.</p>
      <p class="go">Confirm the date &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

  </details>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <details class="sec" id="skies">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>Look Up &middot; Cosmic Events &amp; Dark Skies</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">The other half of aerospace is the sky itself. What is overhead this month, straight from NASA, and the closest dark places to go see it. Checked 2 October 2026.</p>

    <div class="note"><strong>October, from NASA&rsquo;s own What&rsquo;s Up.</strong><br><strong>Sunday 4 October: Saturn at opposition.</strong> It rises around sunset and stays up most of the night, the best Saturn of the year. Look east around 9 p.m.<br><strong>Tuesday 6 October, before sunrise:</strong> a thin crescent Moon right next to Jupiter.<br><strong>Saturday 10 October: new Moon.</strong> That weekend is the dark-sky drive to plan.<br><strong>Night of 21 into 22 October: the Orionids peak</strong>, debris from Halley&rsquo;s Comet. A bright Moon washes out the faint ones, so NASA&rsquo;s tip is to look in the few hours before dawn, after the Moon sets.<br><strong>Night of 27 into 28 October:</strong> the Moon passes the Pleiades. Binoculars help.</div>

    <a class="card" href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/skywatching/whats-up-october-2026-skywatching-tips-from-nasa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">What&rsquo;s Up: October 2026 skywatching</p><span class="pay">Start here</span></div>
      <p class="org">NASA Science, published 1 October</p>
      <p class="why">NASA&rsquo;s own monthly guide, three minutes to read, with sky charts and a short video. The single most reliable calendar of what is visible without equipment.</p>
      <p class="go">Open NASA&rsquo;s October guide &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.com/colorado-stargazing-trail" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Colorado Stargazing Trail</p><span class="pay">The map</span></div>
      <p class="org">colorado.com</p>
      <p class="why">Certified International Dark Sky Parks and dark-sky communities across the state, with a running list of star parties and ranger programs. The single best map for planning a night under real dark skies.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the stargazing trail &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://cpw.state.co.us/placestogo/parks/JacksonLake" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Jackson Lake State Park &middot; closest dark-sky park</p><span class="pay">~1.5 hrs</span></div>
      <p class="org">Colorado Parks &amp; Wildlife</p>
      <p class="loc">Orchard, Colorado, northeast of Denver</p>
      <p class="why">Colorado&rsquo;s first certified Dark Sky Park and the easiest deep-dark sky from the northern Front Range: about ninety minutes from Golden, campsites right there, dark enough to see the faint stuff. The drive to plan is the new Moon weekend of 10 October. Book the site through the official reservation link on this page.</p>
      <p class="go">Open Jackson Lake State Park &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.nps.gov/blca/planyourvisit/astronomy.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Black Canyon of the Gunnison &middot; free ranger astronomy programs</p><span class="pay">~4.5 hrs</span></div>
      <p class="org">National Park Service</p>
      <p class="loc">Montrose area, Colorado</p>
      <p class="why">Certified an International Dark Sky Park in 2015. Rangers, volunteers and the Black Canyon Astronomical Society run free evening programs, solar telescope viewing and constellation tours, and the park never closes so you can stargaze at any hour. Their own tip: Chasm View Overlook on the South Rim is far enough from the road to escape passing headlights.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the park astronomy page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.darkskiescolorado.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Westcliffe &amp; Silver Cliff &middot; free public star parties</p><span class="pay">~3 hrs</span></div>
      <p class="org">Dark Skies of the Wet Mountain Valley, Smokey Jack Observatory</p>
      <p class="loc">Wet Mountain Valley, Colorado</p>
      <p class="why">Two of the highest-altitude Dark Sky Communities in the world, with free star parties and one of the most powerful public telescopes in Colorado. Check their calendar and just show up.</p>
      <p class="go">Open darkskiescolorado.org &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <div class="note">Farther afield, same dark skies: northern New Mexico&rsquo;s <a class="inline" href="https://www.nps.gov/chcu/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#7fa6ff;text-decoration:none;">Chaco Culture</a> is a world-famous Dark Sky Park with night-sky programs (about six hours south), and southern Wyoming&rsquo;s Medicine Bow National Forest and Snowy Range, west of Laramie, are about as dark as it gets, roughly three hours north with no permit needed. Just drive up and look.</div>
  </details>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <details class="sec" id="clubs">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2>Aerospace Clubs at CU</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">The real work this month. These are open student groups inside your own department where you build hardware, and no cold outreach is needed: you turn up to a meeting. Every link re-checked 2 October 2026.</p>

    <div class="note">This is not a side quest. Anduril&rsquo;s 2027 internship lists &ldquo;collegiate project team or laboratory experience&rdquo; under preferred qualifications, SpaceX says its strongest candidates have &ldquo;a history of significant contributions to hands-on extracurricular projects,&rdquo; and Sierra Space says it looks for people who &ldquo;demonstrated their leadership abilities in clubs or on projects.&rdquo; Walking into one of these in September is the thing that makes next fall&rsquo;s applications work. Pick one or two, not five.</div>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/aerospace/current-students/student-groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Smead Aerospace student groups (start here)</p></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">The department&rsquo;s master list of every aerospace student group: rocketry, AIAA, Design Build Fly, SEDS, COBRA, the Flying Club and more. One page, all the doors, and the fallback address for the clubs below whose own sites are broken.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the student-groups page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://cusrl.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Sounding Rocket Lab (CUSRL)</p><span class="pay">Best first stop</span></div>
      <p class="org">Student-run, University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">One of CU&rsquo;s largest student groups, building solid and liquid rockets aimed at the Karman line at 100 km, the edge of space. If you want to machine, test and fly hardware as a first-year, this is the place, and it is the single most legible line on an aerospace r&eacute;sum&eacute; a freshman can get. Their own site is live.</p>
      <p class="go">Open cusrl.com &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/center/spacegrant/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Colorado Space Grant Consortium (COSGC)</p><span class="pay">Paid</span></div>
      <p class="org">NASA-funded, Discovery Learning Center, on campus</p>
      <p class="why">NASA money for students to build and fly real space hardware: CubeSats, balloon payloads, instruments. Headquartered right on the Boulder campus, one of the best hands-on programs in the country, open to undergrads early, and unusually it pays. See the scholarships section above for the current application status.</p>
      <p class="go">Open colorado.edu/center/spacegrant &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/aerospace/current-students/student-groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Colorado Boulder Rocketry Association (COBRA)</p></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">The department&rsquo;s second rocketry group, listed on the Smead student-groups page alongside CUSRL. Between the two of them, CU has more student rocket-building capacity than almost any school in the country.</p>
      <p><strong>Heads up:</strong> the club&rsquo;s own address on the department page is a Google Site that now demands a CU sign-in and shows nothing publicly, so this link goes to the Smead student-groups list instead. Ask at the department or catch them at the fall involvement fair.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Smead student-groups page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/aerospace/current-students/student-groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">AIAA CU Boulder student branch</p><span class="pay">Join for the scholarship clock</span></div>
      <p class="org">American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics</p>
      <p class="why">The professional aerospace society&rsquo;s student chapter. Brings industry engineers, researchers and astronauts in to talk to students, which is a low-pressure way to meet the field through a talk rather than a room. There is a second reason to join now: AIAA Foundation scholarships require a current student membership, so starting it this fall means you are ready for the cycle that opens in October 2027.</p>
      <p><strong>Heads up:</strong> the club&rsquo;s own site is still down and the department page still links the old dead address. This goes to the Smead student-groups list instead. Email the department or turn up to an interest meeting.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Smead student-groups page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/aerospace/current-students/student-groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU SEDS</p></div>
      <p class="org">Students for the Exploration and Development of Space</p>
      <p class="why">A space-focused club open to any major: projects, speakers and competitions for people who just love space. An easy first meeting to attend before you commit to a build team.</p>
      <p><strong>Heads up:</strong> the club&rsquo;s own site is still down and the department page still links the old dead address. This goes to the Smead student-groups list instead.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the Smead student-groups page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://sites.google.com/site/cuboulderdbf/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Design Build Fly (DBF)</p></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">Design, build and fly a radio-controlled aircraft for the national AIAA competition. Pure hands-on aero engineering with a real deadline and a real aircraft at the end, and the closest club on campus to the flying you already do.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the DBF page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://curobotics.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Robotics (ARC competition team)</p></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">A competitive team building industrial-grade robots for the ARC Robotics Competition. No prior robotics experience required, and it splits into design (CAD), build (machining and assembly) and code (autonomy), so you plug into whichever side pulls you. Apply through the form on their site.</p>
      <p class="go">Open curobotics.net &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.corobosub.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Colorado RoboSub</p></div>
      <p class="org">Student-run, University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">Design and build an autonomous submarine and compete in the international RoboSub competition. Open to all experience levels, with mechanical, electrical and software sub-teams, so you can learn ROS, waterproofing and autonomy on a real vehicle. Reach them at robosub@colorado.edu.</p>
      <p class="go">Open corobosub.com &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.hackcu.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">HackCU (student hackathon)</p></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">CU&rsquo;s largest student-run hackathon, with a beginner track and team-forming on site, so no experience is needed. A single weekend rather than a full-season commitment, which makes it the lowest-cost way to build something fast and meet other builders.</p>
      <p class="go">Open hackcu.org &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/ideaforge/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Idea Forge makerspace</p></div>
      <p class="org">College of Engineering, CU Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">The engineering makerspace: machine shop, welding, woodshop, 3D printing and an electronics lab, open to students to build their own projects. Not a club so much as the place where personal aerospace and drone builds actually get fabricated. Start with the workshops and tours. It also pays, see the campus jobs section.</p>
      <p class="go">Open colorado.edu/ideaforge &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.modelaircraft.org/ama-foundation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Academy of Model Aeronautics, local chartered clubs</p><span class="pay">Off campus</span></div>
      <p class="org">AMA Foundation</p>
      <p class="loc">Chartered clubs across the Denver metro and Front Range</p>
      <p class="why">The off-campus community for building and flying model aircraft. Joining a chartered AMA club gets you a legal flying field (see the drone section), and it starts the membership clock the AMA Foundation scholarships require, which is 36 months of membership out of the past 48. Use the club finder in the drone section to pick one near Golden or Boulder.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the AMA Foundation page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
  </details>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <details class="sec" id="outside">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">07</span><h2>Get Outside</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">The other reason you picked Colorado. Sign up for one trip this fall and go. Every link re-checked 2 October 2026.</p>

    <div class="note">Same principle as the build teams: you just get in the van. Rent the gear cheap from the Rec, put your name on one trip, and the rest takes care of itself. One is plenty to start.</div>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/recreation/outdoor-pursuits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Outdoor Pursuits (start here)</p><span class="pay">Start here</span></div>
      <p class="org">Campus Recreation &amp; Well-Being, CU Boulder</p>
      <p class="loc">Student Recreation Center, on campus</p>
      <p class="why">The Rec Center&rsquo;s outdoor hub and the easiest door outside your first semester. Guided trips and skills courses all over Colorado, cheap gear rental (tents, packs, skis, snowshoes), and the on-campus climbing gym, all built with beginner CU students in mind. A Rec membership gets you in.</p>
      <p class="go">Open Outdoor Pursuits &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/recreation/outdoor-pursuits/climbing-gym" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Climbing Gym</p><span class="pay">Climbing</span></div>
      <p class="org">Outdoor Pursuits, CU Boulder</p>
      <p class="loc">Student Recreation Center, on campus</p>
      <p class="why">Over 7,000 square feet of bouldering, top-rope and lead terrain for every level, plus lead and anchor clinics. A pass runs about $35 a semester or $9 a day, shoes $3 a day. Watch a short orientation, sign the waiver, and climb. The lowest-effort way to start, a walk from class.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the climbing gym page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.cuhikingclub.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Hiking Club</p><span class="pay">Since 1919</span></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Hiking Club</p>
      <p class="why">CU&rsquo;s oldest student organization, running day hikes and overnight backpacking trips almost every weekend of the school year. All experience levels, gear help for beginners, and the classic way to see the state on a student budget. Join the email list to get the trip signups. It also puts you at trailheads that double as dark-sky spots.</p>
      <p class="go">Open cuhikingclub.com &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://cualpine.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Alpine Club</p><span class="pay">Climb &amp; alpine</span></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">The all-levels climbing and mountaineering club, from gym rats to big-wallers. They get out nearly every weekend to trad, sport and ice destinations like Indian Creek, the Black Canyon and Ouray, plus clinics and speakers. Trips and signups run through their Discord, so you can lurk before you commit.</p>
      <p class="go">Open cualpine.com &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.cuclimbing.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Boulder Climbing Team</p><span class="pay">Climbing</span></div>
      <p class="org">University of Colorado Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">The competitive side, training through USA Climbing&rsquo;s Collegiate Series and placing second at nationals two years running. Tryouts are in September and weigh character and commitment as much as grade, so it is worth a shot even if you are newer. Practices run at local Spot gyms.</p>
      <p class="go">Open cuclimbing.com &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/sportclubs/co-ed-sports/snowboarding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Snowboarding &amp; Freeski teams</p><span class="pay">Ski &amp; ride</span></div>
      <p class="org">Sport Clubs, CU Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">The snowboard team is 120-plus riders from park rats to chill lappers, with a sister Freeski team on the same sport-clubs page. Both mix riding, filming and a real season-long crew, and the filming side is a straight line to the drone and event video work you already do.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the CU Snowboarding page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://www.colorado.edu/sportclubs/co-ed-sports/cycling" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">CU Cycling &amp; Mountain Bike</p><span class="pay">Bikes</span></div>
      <p class="org">Sport Clubs, CU Boulder</p>
      <p class="why">Around a hundred riders across road, gravel, cyclocross and mountain-bike gravity and endurance, all levels welcome, racing the Rocky Mountain collegiate conference. There is also a laid-back mountain bike group for trail rides and trail-building days if racing is not your thing. Boulder is a world capital for this.</p>
      <p class="go">Open the CU Cycling page &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>
  </details>

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <details class="sec" id="follow">
    <summary class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">08</span><h2>Follow These</h2></summary>
    <p class="sec-sub">Subscribe once and the field comes to you. Free, no networking required. Pick two or three and actually read them. Links re-checked 2 October 2026.</p>

    <a class="card" href="https://payloadspace.com/subscribe/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <div class="card-top"><p class="role">Payload daily newsletter</p><span class="pay">Newsletter</span></div>
      <p class="org">Payload</p>
      <p class="why">Free, short, sharp morning email on the business and policy of space. The easiest way to keep up without much effort, and the fastest route to having something to say in an interview. Sign up once and you are set.</p>
      <p class="go">Subscribe at payloadspace.com &rsaquo;</p>
    </a>

    <a class="card" href="https://spacenews.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
